{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 8, "model_name": "google/gemma-3n-e2b-it", "content": "Raymond III Count of Tripoli was a powerful and influential figure in the 12th century known for his military prowess and political acumen. He ruled the County of Tripoli from 1146 to 1199 solidifying its position as a significant power in the Levant. His reign was marked by both expansionist ambitions and internal challenges.\n\n*Early Life and Ascendancy* Raymond was the son of Raymond I Count of Tripoli and his wife Adela of Garland. He inherited the county upon his father's death in 1146. He quickly proved himself a capable leader engaging in numerous military campaigns to defend Tripoli against Muslim incursions. He strengthened the county's defenses and expanded its territory particularly in the areas of Antioch and Syria. Raymond’s military successes made him a respected figure among his peers and a formidable opponent to his enemies. His strategic thinking and military tactics were widely admired.\n\nRaymond III's reign saw Tripoli become a major maritime power. He established a strong naval force which played a crucial role in controlling trade routes and protecting Tripoli’s coastline. Tripoli’s position as a key trading center facilitated economic growth and brought wealth to the county.  He actively engaged in trade with various European powers and maintained diplomatic relations with numerous rulers. This economic prosperity allowed him to fund his military campaigns and maintain a sizable army.\n\n*Military Campaigns and Conflicts*  Raymond III’s reign was characterized by frequent warfare. He engaged in a series of conflicts with the Seljuk Turks and other Muslim powers vying for control of the Levant.  His most notable military achievement was the successful defense of Tripoli against a massive Seljuk invasion in 1169.  This victory cemented his reputation as a military genius. He also fought against the Normans and other rival powers seeking to expand their influence in the region. These conflicts often involved sieges battles and complex political maneuvering. Raymond’s military campaigns expanded Tripoli’s territory and solidified its position as a regional power.\n\n*Legacy and Decline*  Raymond III’s reign ended in 1199 with his death.  He is remembered as one of the most successful and influential rulers of the County of Tripoli.  His military victories and political skills established Tripoli as a major power in the Levant for several decades.  However the county eventually faced increasing challenges from internal strife and external pressures. The decline of Raymond III’s reign paved the way for the eventual loss of Tripoli's independence to the Ayyubid dynasty in 1289. Despite the eventual loss of power Raymond III’s legacy as a strong and capable ruler remained a significant part of Tripoli’s history.  He is considered a pivotal figure in the history of the Levant and a symbol of resistance against Muslim expansion.\n\n\n\n", "logprobs": [{"token": "Raymond", "logprob": -0.1434580385684967}, {"token": "▁III", "logprob": -1.6689286894688848e-06}, {"token": "▁Count", "logprob": -5.4834770708112046e-05}, {"token": "▁of", "logprob": -2.622600959512056e-06}, {"token": "▁Tripoli", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "▁was", "logprob": -0.09502362459897995}, {"token": "▁a", "logprob": -0.03760075569152832}, {"token": "▁powerful", "logprob": -0.5434863567352295}, {"token": "▁and", "logprob": -1.8260893821716309}, {"token": "▁influential", "logprob": -0.017272407189011574}, {"token": "▁figure", "logprob": -1.003267765045166}, {"token": "▁in", "logprob": -0.033016569912433624}, {"token": "▁the", "logprob": -0.002083513652905822}, {"token": "▁", "logprob": -0.630427360534668}, {"token": "1", "logprob": -1.1920928244535389e-07}, {"token": "2", "logprob": -0.001255200942978263}, {"token": "th", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "▁century", "logprob": -0.02573387883603573}, {"token": "▁known", "logprob": -2.298099040985